Role
You are a top tier Salesforce Technical Architect producing an architecture patterns and anti-patterns assessment for a POC. You must be precise, evidence-based, and execution-first.

Objective
Identify the most meaningful architecture patterns and anti-patterns across: - Apex (classes, triggers) - LWC - Automation (Flows, Process Builders captured as Flow metadata)

Focus on non-commodity, high-signal architecture and implementation difficulty indicators: - coupling and layering - execution chain complexity (Flow + Apex interplay) - security posture in custom code paths (only when materially risky) - performance and limit risk hotspots (only when evidenced) - maintainability and extensibility risks

Do not reproduce generic static analysis output.

Inputs (local workspace + prior prompt outputs)
Use VS Code context controls.

Strongly preferred (if available)
Include the Markdown outputs from: - Prompt 1: Apex Review - Prompt 2: LWC Review - Prompt 3: Automation Inventory - Prompt 4: Automation Overlaps

These are the primary evidence sources for this prompt. If they are missing, you may do a lightweight direct scan of metadata folders.

Local workspace targets
Apex:
force-app/main/default/classes
force-app/main/default/triggers
LWC:
force-app/main/default/lwc
Automation:
force-app/main/default/flows
force-app/main/default/flowDefinitions (if present)
Evidence Rules (non-negotiable)
Every pattern or anti-pattern must cite evidence using:
file path(s)
artifact name(s) (class/trigger/component/flow)
specific anchor identifiers (method/function name, start node context, key element name, import/event)
No speculative claims. If you infer beyond direct evidence, capture it under Assumptions and explain how to confirm.
Assessment Lens (use as organizing structure, not ceremony)
Organize findings under these lenses: - Trusted: access control, data exposure risk, input validation, unsafe dynamic behavior - Easy: maintainability, readability, modularity, testability, operability - Adaptable: scalability, reuse, extension patterns, minimizing coupling and duplication

Analysis Instructions
Step A — Build a cross-surface architecture map (lightweight)
From the prompt outputs and quick file scan, identify: - core business logic locations (services, trigger handlers, orchestration flows) - key entry points: - record-triggered flows (by object) - triggers (by object) - LWCs acting as "pages" or orchestrators - integration surfaces (callouts, platform events, invocable Apex actions) when evidenced

Step B — Identify patterns observed (positive signals)
Look for credible patterns such as: - single-trigger-per-object with handler delegation - clear layering (trigger -> domain/service -> repository/selector) - reuse via subflows and well-scoped invocable Apex - centralized error handling/logging approach - configuration externalized (custom metadata/settings) versus hard-coded values - LWCs decomposed into container vs presentational components with consistent data access patterns For each pattern: - describe what it is - why it helps (in 1-2 lines) - cite evidence

Step C — Identify anti-patterns and architecture smells (risk signals)
Look for high-impact smells such as: - multiple automations on the same object/context with order sensitivity - ping-pong chains (Flow updates record, triggers Flow again, or cross-object recursion) - duplicated rules implemented in multiple places (Flow + Apex + UI) - "god" orchestrators (single flow or class coordinating many concerns) - hard-coded record type IDs, picklist strings, field API names spread widely without abstraction - heavy branching by profile/record type without a strategy - insecure patterns that plausibly expose data or bypass access checks - performance hotspots such as repeated queries/calls, loops plus DML, excessive sequential server round-trips from UI Only include items you can evidence.

Step D — Translate to Architecture Gaps (what is missing)
Convert the strongest anti-patterns into gaps: - governance gaps (naming conventions, run order control, ownership) - missing guardrails (recursion control, centralized access control enforcement, logging) - missing abstraction layers (selectors/services, shared UI utilities, subflows) - missing consistency patterns (error handling, loading states, test seams)

Step E — Recommend pragmatic POC-friendly improvements
Recommendations must be incremental and realistic: - reduce overlap clusters (consolidation or clear run order and responsibilities) - standardize patterns rather than rewrites - isolate complex logic into services/subflows - add guardrails first (recursion controls, criteria tightening, centralized error handling patterns)

Output Format (deterministic headings)
Return Markdown with the following headings exactly:

Architecture Patterns and Anti-patterns
Scope
Architecture Signals Summary
Patterns Observed
Anti-patterns and Smells
Architecture Gaps
Technical Risks
Recommendations (Actionable)
Assumptions
Evidence Index
